[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified the Campus 2 access switch (switch4), its uplink to the distribution switch (sshswitch), and hosts in both affected VLANs. Both VLAN 5 (10.5.0.x) and VLAN 6 (10.6.0.x) are functioning end-to-end.

Mechanism check: A trunk/VLAN tagging issue would prevent hosts from reaching their default gateways on sshswitch. However, from a VLAN 5 host (pc1) I can ping its gateway 10.5.0.254 successfully (0% loss, ~0.57 ms), and from a VLAN 6 host (pc3) I can ping 10.6.0.254 (0% loss, ~0.67 ms). Both hosts also reach upstream 55.5.1.1 (likely the floor/core next-hop) reliably.

Localization/config evidence:
- switch4 shows the uplink subinterfaces eth0.5 and eth0.6 UP and bridged correctly (bridge link: eth0.5 master br_vlan5; eth0.6 master br_vlan6). Access ports are in the right VLAN bridges (eth1/2/3 → br_vlan5; eth4/5 → br_vlan6).
- sshswitch has SVI bridges up for both VLANs with the correct IPs (br_v5 10.5.0.254/24; br_v6 10.6.0.254/24) and matching VLAN-tagged subinterfaces (bond0.5, bond0.6) UP. Its routing table contains OSPF-learned routes upstream, and hosts can reach those paths.

Root cause: No misconfiguration found; the trunk did not “lose” VLAN 5 or 6.

Corrective fix: None required on the network side. If users still see issues, check client IP/gateway/DHCP settings and any local firewalls. Consider that a transient maintenance disruption may have triggered the initial reports; services are now healthy. === END ===